@meta
id: 14-03
title: The whole file in one command
chapter: 14
type: lesson
skills: sub-range, sub-basic
par: 45
minutes: 5
vscode: Ctrl-H, Replace All, and hope
open: order.status.ts

@brief
The status `'PENDING'` is being renamed `'AWAITING_PAYMENT'` because
the product team keep confusing it with "pending fulfilment". Every
occurrence in this file changes: the union type, the constants, the
guards, the comparison in `describe`. Seven in all.

Read the file before you run anything. Confirm for yourself that every
`'PENDING'` here really should change and that nothing else in the file
looks like one. That reading is not a formality. This is the last
lesson in the chapter where a whole-file replace is safe without any
further thought, and you should know why it is safe here.

The range that means "every line" is a single character.

@keys
| `:%s/old/new/g` `Enter` | every line in the file, every match on each line |
| `%` | as a range, all lines — short for `1,$` |
| `:s/old/new/g` `Enter` | for comparison: this line only |

@hints
The command is the same as the last two lessons with one character
added in front of the `s`. That character is a range.
--
`%` means every line. `:%s/…/…/g` is the one substitute most people
learn first and use most.
--
`:%s/'PENDING'/'AWAITING_PAYMENT'/g` and Enter. Include the quotes in
both halves so that the pattern cannot match anything but the literal.

@solution
    :%s/'PENDING'/'AWAITING_PAYMENT'/g<Enter>  :wq<Enter>

Thirty-nine keystrokes, seven changes, and the count would be the same
for seventy.

`%` is a range, the first of several this chapter uses. It is short for
`1,$`, line one to the last line. When you see `:%s` read it as "in the
whole file, substitute". The `g` at the end still means the whole of
each line; the two are independent and you usually want both.

Now the honest part. In this file the rename was safe because you read
it and checked: the literal appears in exactly one meaning, it is
always quoted, and nothing customer-facing is built from it. Most files
are not like that. The word you are renaming appears in a comment where
it should stay, or in a string a customer sees, or inside a longer
identifier where the replacement makes nonsense. Every remaining
lesson in this chapter is about one of the tools for that situation:
narrower ranges, stricter patterns, and a flag that asks you before
each change. Learn `:%s` first, and then learn when not to trust it.

@start order.status.ts
export type OrderStatus = 'PENDING' | 'PAID' | 'SHIPPED' | 'CANCELLED';

export const INITIAL_STATUS: OrderStatus = 'PENDING';

export const OPEN_STATUSES: OrderStatus[] = ['PENDING', 'PAID'];

export const CANCELLABLE_STATUSES: OrderStatus[] = ['PENDING', 'PAID'];

export function isPending(status: OrderStatus) {
  return status === 'PENDING';
}

export function canPay(status: OrderStatus) {
  return status === 'PENDING';
}

export function describe(status: OrderStatus) {
  if (status === 'PENDING') {
    return 'awaiting payment';
  }

  return status.toLowerCase();
}

@expected order.status.ts
export type OrderStatus = 'AWAITING_PAYMENT' | 'PAID' | 'SHIPPED' | 'CANCELLED';

export const INITIAL_STATUS: OrderStatus = 'AWAITING_PAYMENT';

export const OPEN_STATUSES: OrderStatus[] = ['AWAITING_PAYMENT', 'PAID'];

export const CANCELLABLE_STATUSES: OrderStatus[] = ['AWAITING_PAYMENT', 'PAID'];

export function isPending(status: OrderStatus) {
  return status === 'AWAITING_PAYMENT';
}

export function canPay(status: OrderStatus) {
  return status === 'AWAITING_PAYMENT';
}

export function describe(status: OrderStatus) {
  if (status === 'AWAITING_PAYMENT') {
    return 'awaiting payment';
  }

  return status.toLowerCase();
}
